says, in a flat concatenation. Adding a fourth is additive and needs no new stylesheet, so t2's styling instruction is correctly sized.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"p5","ref":"s2/t2","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"DOCS_REVIEW_MARKDOWN_TOKENS maps every markdown-style token to an `--it-*` CSS variable (fg, border, accent, muted, bgInset, codeBg and the font), which is why t2's acceptance check requires the item CSS to reference only `--it-*` names — the chat surface's `--sans`/`--fg-strong`/`--border` namespace is not defined on this surface.","evidence":"DOCS_REVIEW_MARKDOWN_TOKENS is confirmed at markdown-style.ts:120 with `fg: 'var(--it-fg)'` (:123), `border: 'var(--it-border)'` (:126) and `accent: 'var(--it-accent)'` among its members. The broader `var(--it-*)` greps hit high counts across design-tokens.ts, chat-panel.ts and the mocks, which does not weaken the premise — it confirms `--it-*` is the live namespace on this surface. t2's acceptance check (item CSS references only `--it-*`) is therefore a real guard against the S001 failure mode where an undefined var() inside a shorthand silently invalidates the declaration.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"p6","ref":"s2/t2","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing prose renderer buckets a per-item requirement with a missing or empty itemRef under the literal key `(unassigned)`, which is the exact key t2's added acceptance check requires the new renderer to agree with rather than invent its own.","evidence":"Single code match and confirmed read at functional-definition.ts:99: `const key = r.itemRef !== undefined && r.itemRef.length > 0 ? r.itemRef : '(unassigned)';`. t2's added acceptance check names the right literal key, so the new renderer and the existing prose renderer will agree about where an orphan per-item requirement belongs rather than inventing a second convention.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"p7","ref":"s2/t3","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/docs-sections.ts is the single source of section identity and has ZERO import statements, so t3's and t7's acceptance checks that it comes out of this Story byte-identical and still import-free are checks on a real property rather than a restatement.","evidence":"docs-sections.ts is confirmed as the single implementation — deriveSectionIndex at :86 and createSectionResolver at :136, each a single code match, with `const ATX =` at :47 and `const FENCE =` at :50 also single matches. The read at :1 lands on the file's opening doc comment, i.e. the file begins with documentation rather than an import. t3's and t7's 'unchanged and still zero imports' checks therefore guard a real property of a real single-source module.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"p8","ref":"s2/t3","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The format spine declares the functional-requirements section with the literal heading text 'Functional requirements' and `source: 'fr'`, and frBodyLines strips the renderer's own hard-coded heading with `raw.slice(2)` so the engine can supply a NUMBERED one — which is the premise behind t3 matching the anchor title by its TAIL rather than by equality.","evidence":"`heading: 'Functional requirements'` with `source: 'fr'` resolves at formats.ts:36, :58, :79 and :100 — all four document formats — and `raw.slice(2)` is a single match at bindings.ts:27, both confirmed by read. The heading is stripped by the binding precisely so the engine can supply a numbered one, which is exactly why t3 must tail-match the anchor title instead of comparing it for equality. The instruction is grounded in the mechanism that creates the numeric prefix.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"p9","ref":"s2/t4","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"On the degraded path renderMarkdownBody sets `el.className='insrc-docs-plain'` on the BODY CONTAINER, and the shell declares `.insrc-docs-plain{white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;...}` — so t4's added obligation (the item rules must neutralise the inherited white-space/word-break, and the degraded rendering must be checked visually) rests on a real CSS inheritance path, not a hypothetical one.","evidence":"Both halves of the inheritance path are confirmed verbatim: docs-review-panel.ts:90 `el.className='insrc-docs-plain';` followed at :91 by `el.textContent=s;` (the degraded arm sets the class on the BODY CONTAINER), and :327 `.insrc-docs-plain{white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;font-family:var(--it-font);color:var(--it-...)}`. A block prepended inside that container does inherit `white-space:pre-wrap` and `word-break`. This validates the critique that produced t4's added obligation — the item rules must neutralise it, and the degraded path must be checked visually because a DOM stub carries no CSS and would never see it.","action":"none — verified sound; the risk the added acceptance check guards is real","fixability":"auto"},{"claimId":"p10","ref":"s2/t5","kind":"ordering","severity":"LOW","premise":"stampSlugs pairs rendered headings to posted anchors BY TITLE through a pointer that only advances (`var j=p;while(j<anchors.length&&anchors[j].title!==txt)j++;` then `p=j+1`), which is precisely why t5's ordering requirement — placement must run AFTER stamping — is a correctness constraint rather than a style preference: a tree mutated first could mis-pair every later heading.","evidence":"Both patterns are single code matches and both reads confirm: :117 `var j=p;while(j<anchors.length&&anchors[j].title!==txt)j++;` and :118 `if(j<anchors.length){hs[i].id=anchors[j].slug;p=j+1;n++;}}`. The pointer only advances and a heading is stamped only on a title match, so mutating the tree before stamping could genuinely mis-pair every later heading. t5's after-stamping requirement is a correctness constraint